        "date":  "2026-09-18",
        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "MIT Technology Review editors addressed subscriber questions on whether AI poses existential risks, distinguishing between documented near-term harms — weaponized drones in Ukraine, AI-assisted cyberattacks on hospitals — and the significantly less probable scenarios involving complete human extinction that some researchers continue to warn about. The piece serves as a calibration guide for separating credible AI risk from sensationalized threat narratives.",
        "execSummary":  "Enterprise leaders are increasingly asked to take positions on AI existential risk as it surfaces in board discussions, vendor due diligence, and regulatory filings — this MIT TR piece provides a credible, calibrated framing from a respected technical source. The key signal for strategy leaders: documented harms are the near-term governance priority, while extinction-level scenarios remain low-probability but are disproportionately influencing policy. Enterprises should not let existential risk rhetoric crowd out practical AI safety investments like red-teaming, audit trails, and incident response plans. The caveat is that this landscape is shifting fast — what is speculative today is moving toward credible threat status in parallel MIT TR reporting.",
